  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAs mid clouds continue to scatter out and WAA increases, temperatures\nhave been allowed to warm into the mid to upper 70s inland of\nLake Michigan early this afternoon. This already puts several \nlocations within a few degrees of record highs, with several \nhours of heating and deep mixing still on tap. As such, expect \nthat at least a couple of records will be broken by late this \nafternoon. Otherwise, southerly winds continue to ramp up ahead of\napproaching low pressure to the west, with obs currently showing \ngusts to around 20 to 25 mph. Expect gusts to increase to around \n30 mph over the next few hours. Winds then decrease late tonight \nas the pressure gradient loosens up.\n\nThough a couple degrees cooler than today, high temperatures for\ntomorrow are still set to read in the upper 70s to low 80s away\nfrom the Lake. Albeit not as widespread, a couple locations still\nflag for record highs during this time.\n\nRain chances... Light rain still looks on track to arrive late\nMonday afternoon as transient shortwave and weak low pressure\ntraverse the upper Mississippi Valley. Current setup has a high \nPoP (60 to 80%) low QPF look to it, as the parent airmass doesn't\nlook particularly moist. As such, NBM probabilistic guidance only\nshows a 30 to 50% chance for receiving 0.25\" of rain through \nTuesday morning. A rumble or two of thunder wouldn't be out of the\nquestion Monday evening as meager instability (~500 to 600 J/kg \nMUCAPE) builds along the front, though any convective activity \nwould remain elevated with little to no impacts. \n\nNext chances for widespread soaking precip arrive toward the end\nof next week as a deepening surface low and closed upper trough\ntrack from the central Plains into the Midwest. System currently has\na very winter-like appearance to it, especially with potential\nphasing scenario at play if we are to believe the more aggressive\nLREF ensemble clusters. However, there is still a lot of \nvariability this far out in the forecast period, so will continue \nto monitor throughout the week.",
